From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: add V4L2_SEL_TGT_CROP so the scaler can skip coded padding

TOP1_IN_SIZE bounds what the polyphase scaler READS, and it was being handed
the coded size.  H.264 stores 1080p as 1088 rows, so scaling to 720 spent eight
rows of the height budget on encoder padding and shortened the picture by 0.74%.
Because encoders pad by replicating the last row, the visible result is the
bottom edge of the frame smeared to nearly twice its thickness -- measured at 11
panel rows against a nominal 6, both in the decoder's own output and on the
panel.

The decoder interface already names the rectangle needed.  CROP on CAPTURE is
"the rectangle within the coded resolution to be output to CAPTURE", writable on
hardware with compose/scaling capabilities; COMPOSE is "the rectangle inside a
CAPTURE buffer into which the cropped frame is written".  They are the input and
the output of one scaling operation, and only the input was missing.  COMPOSE
keeps exactly the meaning it already had.

So everything that treated src_fmt as the scaler's input now uses ctx->crop,
which defaults to the full coded size: a client that never sets it gets byte
identical output to before.  Setting CROP to 1920x1080 makes the ratio an exact
1.5 on both axes and reproduces a natively rasterised 720p reference border for
border.

Offsets are refused.  TOP1_IN_SIZE bounds the extent the scaler reads, but no
register has been found that moves its origin, so a non-zero left or top could
only be silently ignored -- and a crop that does not crop where it was asked to
is worse than one that admits it cannot.

Two conformance bugs in the COMPOSE family go with it.  COMPOSE_BOUNDS is the
largest rectangle the cropped frame can be composed into, which is the capture
BUFFER and not the coded size once scaling is on; COMPOSE_DEFAULT is defined as
equal to CROP.  Both returned src_fmt.  G_SELECTION also refused every target
unless the hardware could scale, where the interface says such targets are
read-only rather than absent.

Validated on an H713.  The CROP path reproduces a register-level override of
TOP1_IN_SIZE byte for byte (md5 a1b57792782154dadf21a4eb1da64a77), and a crop
equal to the coded size is byte identical to no crop at all
(3fb46599f06f6fc27aaa0b3ca420ac92) -- the control that separates "the register
works" from "touching this perturbs something".  v4l2-compliance stays 49/49
with zero warnings, and now reports Cropping and Composing as OK where both
previously read "Not Supported".  cedrus-scaler-api-test and cedrus-control-test
pass unchanged, as do the three decode gates.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Origin: https://github.com/cstenger/allwinner-h713-mainline/blob/43b2cdd4aa1b161a281db8b10f02ce70085e2d42/patches/kernel/0122-media-cedrus-add-sel-tgt-crop-for-the-scaler-input.patch (0122-media-cedrus-add-sel-tgt-crop-for-the-scaler-input.patch, commit 10ffaad)
---
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
@@ -145,6 +145,18 @@
 	 * picture userspace dequeues.
 	 */
 	struct v4l2_pix_format		recon_fmt;
+	/*
+	 * The two rectangles of one scaling operation, as the decoder interface
+	 * names them: crop is what the scaler READS out of the coded frame,
+	 * compose is where it WRITES inside the capture buffer.
+	 *
+	 * crop exists so a client can exclude coded padding.  H.264 stores
+	 * 1080p as 1088 rows, and scaling all 1088 into 720 puts eight rows of
+	 * encoder padding into the picture and shortens everything else by
+	 * 0.74%.  Defaults to the full coded size, so a client that never sets
+	 * it sees exactly the old behaviour.
+	 */
+	struct v4l2_rect		crop;
 	struct v4l2_rect		compose;
 	void				*scaler_buf;
 	dma_addr_t			scaler_buf_addr;
@@ -287,10 +299,17 @@
 		ctx->src_fmt.pixelformat == V4L2_PIX_FMT_HEVC_SLICE);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The secondary output is in use whenever what userspace dequeues is not simply
+ * the whole coded frame -- because it was scaled, or because it was cropped, or
+ * both.
+ */
 static inline bool cedrus_scaling(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx)
 {
 	return ctx->compose.width != ctx->src_fmt.width ||
-	       ctx->compose.height != ctx->src_fmt.height;
+	       ctx->compose.height != ctx->src_fmt.height ||
+	       ctx->crop.width != ctx->src_fmt.width ||
+	       ctx->crop.height != ctx->src_fmt.height;
 }
 
 /*
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
@@ -326,8 +326,15 @@
 		cedrus_write(dev, VE_CHROMA_BUF_LEN, reg);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * TOP1_IN_SIZE bounds what the scaler READS, so the crop rectangle --
+	 * not the coded size -- is its input. Measured: feeding it 1080 instead
+	 * of H.264's 1088 coded rows reproduces a natively rasterised 720p
+	 * reference border for border, against a bottom border nearly twice too
+	 * thick when the padding is scaled in with the picture.
+	 */
 	cedrus_top1_configure(ctx, ctx->scaler_buf_addr,
-			      ctx->src_fmt.width, ctx->src_fmt.height,
+			      ctx->crop.width, ctx->crop.height,
 			      ctx->compose.width, ctx->compose.height);
 }
 
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
@@ -250,11 +250,11 @@
 
 	if (cedrus_can_scale(ctx) && fsize->pixel_format == V4L2_PIX_FMT_NV12) {
 		fsize->type = V4L2_FRMSIZE_TYPE_STEPWISE;
-		fsize->stepwise.min_width = cedrus_scale_min(ctx->src_fmt.width);
-		fsize->stepwise.max_width = ctx->src_fmt.width & ~1U;
+		fsize->stepwise.min_width = cedrus_scale_min(ctx->crop.width);
+		fsize->stepwise.max_width = ctx->crop.width & ~1U;
 		fsize->stepwise.step_width = 2;
-		fsize->stepwise.min_height = cedrus_scale_min(ctx->src_fmt.height);
-		fsize->stepwise.max_height = ALIGN(ctx->src_fmt.height, 16);
+		fsize->stepwise.min_height = cedrus_scale_min(ctx->crop.height);
+		fsize->stepwise.max_height = ALIGN(ctx->crop.height, 16);
 		fsize->stepwise.step_height = 2;
 	} else {
 		struct v4l2_pix_format pix_fmt = ctx->src_fmt;
@@ -376,15 +376,17 @@
 
 	pix_fmt->pixelformat = fmt->pixelformat;
 	if (cedrus_can_scale(ctx) && pix_fmt->pixelformat == V4L2_PIX_FMT_NV12) {
-		pix_fmt->width = cedrus_scale_size(ctx->src_fmt.width,
+		pix_fmt->width = cedrus_scale_size(ctx->crop.width,
 						 pix_fmt->width, 0);
-		pix_fmt->height = cedrus_scale_size(ctx->src_fmt.height,
+		pix_fmt->height = cedrus_scale_size(ctx->crop.height,
 						  pix_fmt->height, 0);
 		scaling = pix_fmt->width != ctx->src_fmt.width ||
-			  pix_fmt->height != ctx->src_fmt.height;
+			  pix_fmt->height != ctx->src_fmt.height ||
+			  ctx->crop.width != ctx->src_fmt.width ||
+			  ctx->crop.height != ctx->src_fmt.height;
 	} else {
-		pix_fmt->width = ctx->src_fmt.width;
-		pix_fmt->height = ctx->src_fmt.height;
+		pix_fmt->width = ctx->crop.width;
+		pix_fmt->height = ctx->crop.height;
 	}
 	cedrus_prepare_capture_format(ctx, pix_fmt, scaling);
 	return 0;
@@ -443,32 +445,120 @@
 			ctx->current_codec->extra_cap_size(ctx, &ctx->recon_fmt);
 }
 
+/*
+ * CAPTURE selection, as the decoder interface defines it: CROP is the rectangle
+ * within the CODED resolution that is output, and COMPOSE is the rectangle
+ * inside the capture BUFFER it is written to. They are the input and the output
+ * of the same scaling operation.
+ *
+ * The targets are readable whatever the hardware can do -- the interface says
+ * they are read-only, not absent, without compose/scaling support -- so a
+ * client can discover the coded size here even on a context that cannot scale.
+ * Writing them is what requires the scaler; see cedrus_s_selection().
+ */
 static int cedrus_g_selection(struct file *file, void *priv,
 			      struct v4l2_selection *sel)
 {
 	struct cedrus_ctx *ctx = cedrus_file2ctx(file);
 
-	if (sel->type != V4L2_BUF_TYPE_VIDEO_CAPTURE ||
-	    !cedrus_can_scale(ctx) ||
-	    !cedrus_is_capable(ctx, CEDRUS_CAPABILITY_UNTILED))
+	if (sel->type != V4L2_BUF_TYPE_VIDEO_CAPTURE)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	switch (sel->target) {
+	case V4L2_SEL_TGT_CROP:
+		sel->r = ctx->crop;
+		return 0;
+	case V4L2_SEL_TGT_CROP_DEFAULT:
+	case V4L2_SEL_TGT_CROP_BOUNDS:
+		/*
+		 * CROP_BOUNDS is the coded resolution. CROP_DEFAULT should be
+		 * the VISIBLE rectangle, which a stateless decoder cannot know:
+		 * userspace parses the stream, and neither v4l2_ctrl_h264_sps
+		 * nor v4l2_ctrl_hevc_sps carries the cropping fields. Reporting
+		 * the coded size is the honest answer, and userspace narrows it
+		 * with S_SELECTION(CROP) when it knows better.
+		 */
+		sel->r.left = 0;
+		sel->r.top = 0;
+		sel->r.width = ctx->src_fmt.width;
+		sel->r.height = ctx->src_fmt.height;
+		return 0;
 	case V4L2_SEL_TGT_COMPOSE:
 		sel->r = ctx->compose;
 		return 0;
 	case V4L2_SEL_TGT_COMPOSE_DEFAULT:
+		/* "equal to V4L2_SEL_TGT_CROP", placed at the buffer origin. */
+		sel->r.left = 0;
+		sel->r.top = 0;
+		sel->r.width = ctx->crop.width;
+		sel->r.height = ctx->crop.height;
+		return 0;
 	case V4L2_SEL_TGT_COMPOSE_BOUNDS:
+	case V4L2_SEL_TGT_COMPOSE_PADDED:
+		/*
+		 * The largest rectangle the cropped frame can be composed into
+		 * is the capture BUFFER, which is not the coded size once
+		 * scaling is on -- reporting the coded size here was wrong.
+		 */
 		sel->r.left = 0;
 		sel->r.top = 0;
-		sel->r.width = ctx->src_fmt.width;
-		sel->r.height = ctx->src_fmt.height;
+		sel->r.width = ctx->dst_fmt.width;
+		sel->r.height = ctx->dst_fmt.height;
 		return 0;
 	default:
 		return -EINVAL;
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Narrow what the scaler reads out of the coded frame.
+ *
+ * This is how a client excludes coded padding: H.264 stores 1080p as 1088 rows,
+ * and scaling all 1088 into 720 spends eight rows of the height budget on
+ * encoder padding and shortens the picture by 0.74%. Setting CROP to 1920x1080
+ * makes the ratio exact on both axes.
+ *
+ * Offsets are refused. TOP1_IN_SIZE bounds the extent the scaler reads but no
+ * register has been found that moves its origin, so a non-zero left or top
+ * would have to be silently ignored, and a crop that does not crop where it was
+ * asked to is worse than one that says it cannot.
+ */
+static int cedrus_s_selection_crop(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx,
+				   struct v4l2_selection *sel)
+{
+	struct v4l2_rect crop = sel->r;
+
+	if (crop.left || crop.top || !crop.width || !crop.height ||
+	    crop.width > INT_MAX || crop.height > INT_MAX)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/* Even, and never larger than the coded frame the decoder produced. */
+	crop.width = clamp(crop.width & ~1U, 16U, ctx->src_fmt.width & ~1U);
+	crop.height = clamp(crop.height & ~1U, 16U, ctx->src_fmt.height & ~1U);
+
+	if (((sel->flags & V4L2_SEL_FLAG_LE) &&
+	     (crop.width > sel->r.width || crop.height > sel->r.height)) ||
+	    ((sel->flags & V4L2_SEL_FLAG_GE) &&
+	     (crop.width < sel->r.width || crop.height < sel->r.height)))
+		return -ERANGE;
+
+	ctx->crop = crop;
+
+	/*
+	 * The output rectangle was clamped against the OLD input, so re-derive
+	 * it: a compose that is now larger than the crop, or more than the 4x
+	 * the scaler is validated for below it, is no longer reachable.
+	 */
+	ctx->compose.width = cedrus_scale_size(crop.width, ctx->compose.width, 0);
+	ctx->compose.height = cedrus_scale_size(crop.height, ctx->compose.height, 0);
+	ctx->dst_fmt.width = max_t(u32, ctx->dst_fmt.width, ctx->compose.width);
+	ctx->dst_fmt.height = max_t(u32, ctx->dst_fmt.height, ctx->compose.height);
+	cedrus_reset_cap_format(ctx);
+	cedrus_update_recon_format(ctx);
+	sel->r = crop;
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static int cedrus_s_selection(struct file *file, void *priv,
 			      struct v4l2_selection *sel)
 {
@@ -477,7 +567,9 @@
 	struct v4l2_rect compose = sel->r;
 
 	if (sel->type != V4L2_BUF_TYPE_VIDEO_CAPTURE ||
-	    sel->target != V4L2_SEL_TGT_COMPOSE || !cedrus_can_scale(ctx) ||
+	    (sel->target != V4L2_SEL_TGT_COMPOSE &&
+	     sel->target != V4L2_SEL_TGT_CROP) ||
+	    !cedrus_can_scale(ctx) ||
 	    !cedrus_is_capable(ctx, CEDRUS_CAPABILITY_UNTILED) ||
 	    sel->flags & ~(V4L2_SEL_FLAG_LE | V4L2_SEL_FLAG_GE))
 		return -EINVAL;
@@ -485,12 +577,16 @@
 	vq = v4l2_m2m_get_vq(ctx->fh.m2m_ctx, V4L2_BUF_TYPE_VIDEO_CAPTURE);
 	if (vb2_is_busy(vq))
 		return -EBUSY;
+
+	if (sel->target == V4L2_SEL_TGT_CROP)
+		return cedrus_s_selection_crop(ctx, sel);
+
 	if (compose.left || compose.top || !compose.width || !compose.height ||
 	    compose.width > INT_MAX || compose.height > INT_MAX)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
-	compose.width = cedrus_scale_size(ctx->src_fmt.width, compose.width, sel->flags);
-	compose.height = cedrus_scale_size(ctx->src_fmt.height, compose.height, sel->flags);
+	compose.width = cedrus_scale_size(ctx->crop.width, compose.width, sel->flags);
+	compose.height = cedrus_scale_size(ctx->crop.height, compose.height, sel->flags);
 	if (((sel->flags & V4L2_SEL_FLAG_LE) &&
 	     (compose.width > sel->r.width || compose.height > sel->r.height)) ||
 	    ((sel->flags & V4L2_SEL_FLAG_GE) &&
@@ -524,8 +620,8 @@
 
 	ctx->dst_fmt = f->fmt.pix;
 	ctx->compose = (struct v4l2_rect) {
-		.width = min(f->fmt.pix.width, ctx->src_fmt.width),
-		.height = min(f->fmt.pix.height, ctx->src_fmt.height),
+		.width = min(f->fmt.pix.width, ctx->crop.width),
+		.height = min(f->fmt.pix.height, ctx->crop.height),
 	};
 	cedrus_update_recon_format(ctx);
 
@@ -542,8 +638,8 @@
 	ctx->dst_fmt.sizeimage = 0;
 	if (!cedrus_scaling(ctx)) {
 		ctx->dst_fmt.bytesperline = 0;
-		ctx->dst_fmt.width = ctx->src_fmt.width;
-		ctx->dst_fmt.height = ctx->src_fmt.height;
+		ctx->dst_fmt.width = ctx->crop.width;
+		ctx->dst_fmt.height = ctx->crop.height;
 	}
 	cedrus_prepare_capture_format(ctx, &ctx->dst_fmt, cedrus_scaling(ctx));
 }
@@ -598,10 +694,11 @@
 	 * A new coded size invalidates any scaling factor chosen against the
 	 * old one, and the reconstruction geometry follows the source.
 	 */
-	ctx->compose = (struct v4l2_rect) {
+	ctx->crop = (struct v4l2_rect) {
 		.width = ctx->src_fmt.width,
 		.height = ctx->src_fmt.height,
 	};
+	ctx->compose = ctx->crop;
 	ctx->dst_fmt.pixelformat = 0;
 	ctx->dst_fmt.bytesperline = 0;
 	cedrus_reset_cap_format(ctx);
